划分为256个区间，每个区间对应一个二进制数，以PaLM-E为骨干的模型再将256个二进制数替换为256个使用频率最低的生僻字，从而将运动信息转为语言信息置入LLM 
模型中训练；以PaLI-X为骨干的模型具备1000个独立Token，二进制数序列可直接置入模型训练。”；关于SayCan实验结果，报告指出：“在实验中，SayCan在真实世界的
厨房环境中执行了101项任务，规划成功率为81%，执行成功率为60%，比未经过现实世界约束的LLMs执行准确性提高了约15%。”；报告对RT-1测试结果的描述：“RT-1可 
在家庭厨房场景中执行超过700项具体任务，在3000多次真实测试中平均成功率达97%，典型操作如‘移动物品’‘打开抽屉’等成功率超过90%。”。[2][15]              

现有公开资料显示：Global embodied AI research describes four main technical routes for large‑model‑driven robots: (1) modular hierarchical         
architectures, (2) hierarchical architectures with large models in specific modules, (3) end‑to‑end large multimodal models directly mapping       
perception to actions, and (4) world‑model‑based approaches that learn predictive models of the environment.；Traditional embodied intelligence    
control frameworks for robots are commonly categorized into imitation learning, reinforcement learning, and model predictive control, each with    
characteristic data and sample efficiency limitations that motivate the integration of large models.；Large-model-driven embodied intelligence is  
framed as a cross-disciplinary route that combines the perceptual, reasoning, and logical capabilities of large language and multimodal models with
traditional robot learning methods (imitation learning, reinforcement learning, and model predictive control) to improve data efficiency and       
generalization in physical interaction tasks.；End‑to‑end visual‑language‑action (VLA) large models for robots currently fail to realize a robust  
closed loop of perception–decision–execution in real environments, exhibiting limited generalization outside their training distributions and poor 
performance on long‑horizon tasks, with reported success rates around 20–40% on complex sequences such as trash sorting and item                   
exchange.。[2][13][4][15]                                                                                                                          

现有公开资料显示：Hierarchical large‑model routes for robots typically use large language or multimodal models for task planning and high‑level    
decision making (e.g., SayCan, Inner Monologue, Gemini Robotics‑ER 1.5), combined with separate low‑level controllers for continuous control,      
yielding higher planning reliability and execution accuracy than using unstructured LLMs alone in real‑world tasks such as kitchen                 
manipulation.；Robot Transformer–style multimodal large models (e.g., RT‑1, RT‑2, PaLM‑E, π0 series, Helix) constitute a technical route where     
perception and language are fused with learned action policies, enabling robots to perform hundreds of distinct manipulation tasks and to          
generalize to novel instructions in household environments, with reported success rates up to ~97% on diverse kitchen tasks for                    
RT‑1.；World‑model‑based routes in embodied AI leverage predictive models of physical environments, such as NVIDIA’s Cosmos integrated in Isaac Sim
and Omniverse pipelines, to generate large‑scale, photorealistic, physics‑consistent synthetic data for robot and autonomous driving training,     
aiming to alleviate real‑world data scarcity.；Simulation engines for embodied AI data generation are classified into (1) general-purpose          
physics-based platforms (e.g., Gazebo, Isaac Sim) and (2) realistic scene reconstruction platforms (e.g., AI2‑THOR, iGibson), which together       
underpin routes that combine simulated and real robot data for safe training and evaluation.。[4][15][2][13]                                       

现有公开资料显示：Data-scaling laws emerging from embodied AI studies such as ManiBox and GEN‑0 indicate that for spatial generalization the       
required data volume grows roughly as a power of the expansion in spatial volume (e.g., ~38^0.35 for 38× larger space), and that increasing        
training data by 10× can yield approximately proportional performance gains for certain generalist embodied models.；Embodied interaction methods  
are commonly categorized into (1) human–agent interaction, including embodied question answering systems in visual environments (e.g., EQAv1,      
iGQA), and (2) agent–object interaction, including language‑guided manipulation algorithms that couple large language models with control          
strategies (e.g., SayCan, Code‑as‑Policies).。[2][4]                                                                                               

